\begin{abstract}
We study a compact, hardware-matched variational quantum circuit (VQC) for Max-Cut and illustrate it on a five-qubit instance executed on a trapped-ion quantum processor in the accompanying source document. Our ansatz comprises two layers of single-qubit rotations separated by a fixed, problem-independent entangling layer reflecting ion-trap connectivity. Parameters are trained with a classical optimizer using the parameter-shift rule, and the cost is evaluated as the expectation of the Ising cut Hamiltonian from measured bitstring probabilities. On the documented five-node graph, the ion-trap experiment concentrated roughly 95\% of the probability mass on the theoretically optimal cuts. In a noiseless state-vector simulator, our implementation recovers the exact optimum cut value and concentrates essentially all probability on the same four optimal bit strings. We enhance the original write-up with (i) a detailed methodological exposition, (ii) complexity and sample cost analysis, (iii) ablations of entangling topology and depth, (iv) baseline comparisons against classical heuristics and first-layer QAOA, (v) multi-seed statistical reporting with confidence intervals and significance testing, and (vi) a fully reproducible artifact with an extensible, dependency-light reference implementation. We discuss limitations, including scaling to larger graphs under noise and trainability challenges (e.g., barren plateaus), and we outline directions for algorithm–hardware co-design in the trapped-ion setting. Our results underscore that modest-depth, hardware-aligned circuits can attain optimal Max-Cut on small instances while serving as realistic sandboxes for NISQ-era research.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Combinatorial optimization underpins crucial tasks in communications, logistics, and machine learning. Among canonical NP-hard problems, Max-Cut is notably central, with applications in circuit partitioning, clustering, and resource allocation. For a graph $G=(V,E)$, the objective is to partition vertices into two sets to maximize the number (or total weight) of edges crossing the partition. Despite mature classical algorithms, exact solutions remain intractable in the worst case \cite{garey1979}, and even high-quality approximations can be challenging on specific instance families. The seminal Goemans–Williamson algorithm offers a celebrated 0.878-approximation guarantee via semidefinite programming (SDP) and randomized hyperplane rounding \cite{goemans1995maxcut}, yet its SDP step may be costly for large instances or constrained deployments.

Variational quantum algorithms (VQAs) target near-term noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) devices by combining shallow parameterized quantum circuits with classical optimization \cite{preskill2018nisq,cerezo2021variational,tilly2022vqe}. The Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) \cite{farhi2014qaoa} and the Variational Quantum Eigensolver (VQE) \cite{peruzzo2014vqe} represent archetypal frameworks. In principle, coherent interference across Hilbert space may enable expressivity patterns distinct from classical heuristics, while shallow circuits and problem-tailored hardware-efficient ans\"atze can mitigate decoherence and control errors \cite{kandala2017hardware}. However, VQA practicality hinges on trainability, measurement overhead, and ansatz–hardware co-design \cite{mcclean2018barren,arrasmith2021trainability,wang2021noiseplateaus}.

This paper investigates a minimal, hardware-matched circuit for a five-qubit Max-Cut instance tested on a trapped-ion system in the provided source. Ion traps support all-to-all connectivity with high-fidelity entangling gates and long coherence times \cite{bruzeiwicz2019trappedion,pino2021qccd,wright2019benchmarking}. We employ a two-layer rotation–entangler–rotation template with a fixed entangling layer (CZ ring by default), train parameters using the parameter-shift rule \cite{schuld2019gradients,mitarai2018quantum}, and compute the cost from measured outcome probabilities exploiting that the Max-Cut Hamiltonian is diagonal in the computational basis. Our simulator (NumPy-only) achieves the exact optimum cost and concentrates nearly all probability on the four optimal bitstrings reported by the ion-trap study.

Contributions:
- We formalize a hardware-matched VQC for Max-Cut on trapped-ion devices and provide explicit, reproducible training via parameter-shift gradients.
- We add rigorous complexity and sampling analyses for both state-vector simulation and hardware execution.
- We conduct ablations on entangler topology and depth, and we compare against classical baselines (random, greedy with local search, simulated annealing) and a first-layer QAOA comparator \cite{farhi2014qaoa,hadfield2019qaoaops}.
- We provide multi-seed statistical reporting, confidence intervals, and paired significance tests on a small benchmark.
- We release a concise reference implementation designed for extensibility and replication.

