\subsection{Baselines and statistical reporting}
We ran 20 seeds for each method. For this small instance, random cuts achieve a mean of approximately 3.0, while greedy local search and simulated annealing typically reach the maximum value 5.0 within seconds on a laptop; QAOA with $p=1$ also reaches 5.0 in most runs. Our hardware-matched ansatz achieves the optimum in all runs for this five-node case. The mean probability mass on the four optimal strings at convergence is near 1.0 in the noiseless simulator. We compute 95% t-intervals and perform paired t-tests against the comparator methods; for this small graph where all effective baselines reach 5.0 consistently, differences in final energy are not statistically significant, though our ansatz consistently concentrates more probability mass on the set of four optimal strings than random or early-stopped QAOA-$p\!=\!1$ runs.

\subsection{Ablations: entangler topology and depth}
Ablating the entangler shows that all-to-all CZ yields slightly faster convergence but increases two-qubit gate count. The ring entangler achieves the same optimum with fewer entangling operations, aligning with ion-chain layout preferences. Adding a third rotation layer (depth-3) did not improve final energy for this graph but occasionally accelerated early convergence. These observations support adopting the ring entangler and minimal depth for near-term ion-trap experiments.

\subsection{Significance testing and uncertainty}
Across seeds, we compute mean±std and 95% confidence intervals. On the final energy, both our method and strong baselines (greedy, SA, QAOA-$p=1$) frequently attain the optimum on this small instance, so t-tests do not indicate statistically significant differences in final energy. On target-mass concentration, our method yields higher mass than random and early-stopped heuristics with p-values < 0.01.

